        <div id="what-10kw-runs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What a 10,000 Watt Generator Actually Runs
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start With the Running Number</h3>

          <p>
            Start with the honest number. A generator marketed as 10,000 watts delivers 8,000 to 9,000 running watts on gasoline. That is the sustained capacity you budget against, and it is a lot of power: roughly a third of what a typical house draws at its busiest moment, and far more than most homes use on an ordinary outage evening when nobody is cooking or doing laundry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What 8,000 to 9,000 Watts Covers</h3>

          <p>
            In practical terms, 8,000 to 9,000 running watts covers a full essentials list at once. A refrigerator and a chest freezer together average a few hundred watts each once running. A gas furnace blower adds 600 to 800. Lights, internet, phone chargers and a television are a few hundred more. A 1 HP well pump runs on about 1,000 to 1,200 watts. Add a sump pump and a microwave and you are still around 5,000 to 6,000 running watts, which leaves real headroom for motor surges.
          </p>

          <p>
            That headroom is what separates this class from a 5,000 or 7,500 watt generator. Smaller units can run the same appliances, but only if you stage them carefully so two motors never start at the same moment. At 10kW you mostly stop thinking about it. The well pump can kick on while the furnace is running and the fridge compressor cycles, and the generator absorbs it. Our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> lists running and starting figures for the common household loads if you want to total your own.
          </p>

