            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Does Not Do</h3>

          <p>
            What it does not do is run everything at once. Central air, an electric dryer, an electric range and an electric water heater are each multi-kilowatt loads, and a 10kW generator handles one of them at a time alongside your essentials, not all of them together. If your plan requires two of those simultaneously, you are shopping for the <a href="/best-12000-watt-generator/">12,000 watt class</a> or a standby unit, and the rest of this guide will help you see which.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="peak-vs-running" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Peak Watts vs Running Watts: Reading the Box Honestly
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Peak vs Running Watts</h3>

          <p>
            Generator naming is a marketing contest, and the starting watt figure wins it. Starting or peak watts describe what the alternator can deliver for a few seconds while a motor spins up. Running watts describe what it can deliver for hours. The gap is usually 20 to 30 percent. Look at our picks: three of them carry 11,000 or 12,000 in their names, yet all three are rated for exactly 9,000 running watts on gasoline.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the Westinghouse iGen12000DFc sits on a 10,000 watt list. Westinghouse prints 12,000 on the box because that is its peak, but its running rating matches the Champion 201417 and DuroMax XP11000iH watt for watt. Compare generators on running watts and they fall into neat classes. Compare them on peak watts and you will overpay for a label.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fuel Changes the Number</h3>

          <p>
            Fuel changes the number too. Propane carries less energy per gallon than gasoline, so dual fuel generators are rated lower on LP. Across our picks the propane derate runs from about 5 percent on the DuroMax to 10 percent on the Champion and Westinghouse. Natural gas is lower still: the tri-fuel Champion 201423 drops from 9,000 running watts on gasoline to 7,290 on natural gas, a loss of almost a fifth. If you intend to run on propane or natural gas, use those ratings when you size, not the gasoline figure.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Size on running watts, on the fuel you will actually burn.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Total the running watts of everything you want on at once, add the single largest starting surge, then compare against the generator's running rating for propane or natural gas if that is your fuel. The peak number on the box is only useful for checking that one big motor will start.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Altitude and Heat</h3>

          <p>
            One more catch: altitude and heat. Air-cooled engines lose power as air thins, commonly cited at around 3.5 percent per 1,000 feet above sea level. At 5,000 feet a 9,000 watt generator behaves more like a 7,500 watt one. Owners in mountain states routinely report needing one size larger than the sea-level math suggests, and manufacturers' manuals describe the same derate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="well-pump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The 240V Well Pump Test
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Pump Runs on 240 Volts</h3>

          <p>
            For rural buyers the well pump is the reason to own a 10kW generator. Without it there is no running water, no flushing toilets and no way to fight a fire. Most residential submersible pumps of 3/4 HP and up run on 240 volts, so the first requirement is not watts at all: the generator needs true 120/240V output. Every pick on this list has it. Many smaller inverters do not, and no amount of wattage helps a 120V-only machine start a 240V pump.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Starting Surge</h3>

          <p>
            Next comes the surge. A 1 HP submersible runs on about 1,000 to 1,200 watts but needs 3,000 to 4,000 watts for the second or two it takes to start, and deeper wells push both numbers up. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-well-pump/">well pump sizing guide</a> walks through how pump depth and pressure settings affect that figure. The point here is that a 1 HP pump starting on top of a fridge, freezer, furnace blower and lights lands around 7,000 to 8,000 watts at the moment of startup. A 7,500 watt generator manages that with no margin. A 9,000 running-watt unit does it with room to spare.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hardwired Means a Transfer Switch</h3>

          <p>
            Well pumps are also hardwired. There is no plug to move to an extension cord, so the pump has to be fed through a transfer switch or an interlock with an inlet box. That makes the generator's 240V outlet the critical spec, which is why we come back to 30-amp versus 50-amp outlets below. A 1.5 HP or 2 HP pump is where a 10kW generator starts to feel tight, especially on propane; at that pump size, check the control box data plate and lean toward the top of the class.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Load Juggling</h3>

          <p>
            There is one more practical benefit to the extra capacity. With a 10kW generator you do not have to manage the pump. It cycles when the pressure switch tells it to, regardless of what else is running, and that is what living through a multi-day outage on a well actually requires. Owners stepping up from 5,000 watt machines frequently say this is the change they notice most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="central-air" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Central Air: Possible, but Only With a Soft Starter
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Locked-Rotor Problem</h3>

          <p>
            This is where 10kW buyers most often get disappointed. Central air conditioners do not run on much power once started. A three-ton system typically draws 3,000 to 3,800 running watts. The problem is the compressor's locked-rotor surge, which can reach 9,000 to 12,000 watts on its own. That is the entire peak output of a 10,000 watt generator before the refrigerator or anything else is connected, and the result is a stalled compressor, a tripped breaker or a generator that bogs and shuts down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Soft Starter Fix</h3>

          <p>
            A smaller two-ton unit may start on a 10kW generator with very little else running, but it is marginal and owners report mixed results. The reliable fix is a soft starter fitted to the condenser. It ramps the compressor up over a fraction of a second instead of slamming it with full voltage, and it commonly cuts a three-ton unit's starting draw to around 3,000 to 5,000 watts. That moves central air from impossible to comfortable on any generator in this guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The arithmetic with a soft starter looks like this: a three-ton system at about 3,500 running watts, a refrigerator, a furnace blower or air handler, lights and internet together come to roughly 5,500 running watts. Add a soft-started compressor surge of 3,000 to 5,000 watts and the peak sits around 8,500 to 10,500, which a 9,000 running-watt inverter with 11,000 starting watts can handle. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-central-air-conditioning/">central air sizing guide</a> has the full tonnage table.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WORTH KNOWING</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A soft starter is often cheaper than the generator upgrade it avoids.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The device and installation typically cost a fraction of the price difference between a 10kW portable and a 12-15kW portable or a standby unit. If cooling is the reason you are shopping big, price a soft starter first.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Still One Big Load at a Time</h3>

          <p>
            Even with a soft starter, do not plan to run central air and an electric dryer or water heater at the same time. Two multi-kilowatt loads plus essentials exceed what this class can carry. Pick the one that matters, usually cooling in a summer outage, and leave the others off until the grid comes back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="big-240v-loads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Dryers, Ranges and Water Heaters: One at a Time
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Resistive Loads Have No Surge</h3>

          <p>
            Big resistive loads are easier on a generator than motors because they have no starting surge: they pull the same wattage the moment they switch on. They are just large. An electric dryer draws about 5,000 to 5,600 watts, an electric water heater about 4,500, a single large range burner around 2,500 and an oven 2,000 to 5,000 depending on its element. Each one fits on a 10kW generator. Any two of them together, plus essentials, generally do not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rotate the Big Loads</h3>

          <p>
            The workable approach is load rotation. Run the water heater for an hour in the morning to bring the tank up to temperature, then switch it off at the breaker. Run the dryer in the afternoon. Cook on one or two burners rather than the oven. A 50-gallon water heater holds heat for many hours once charged, so you lose very little by cycling it. Our guide on whether a <a href="/can-a-portable-generator-power-an-electric-water-heater/">portable generator can power an electric water heater</a> covers that routine in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Planning Transfer Switch Circuits</h3>

          <p>
            If you are wiring the generator in through a manual transfer switch, choose which 240V circuits it can reach with this in mind. Many installers put the well pump, the furnace and one big appliance circuit on the generator side and leave the rest off. If you are using an interlock on the main panel, every circuit is available, which is more flexible but puts the burden on you to switch the large loads off before you start the generator.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electronic Controls on Ranges</h3>

          <p>
            One caution about ranges and ovens with electronic controls: they are generally fine on inverter output and usually fine on open-frame generators, but owners occasionally report display flicker or error codes on generators with higher harmonic distortion. If your kitchen is full of electronic controls and you plan to cook on generator power, that is another small argument for one of the inverter picks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="30a-vs-50a" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. 30-Amp vs 50-Amp: The Outlet That Caps Your Generator
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One Outlet Sets the Ceiling</h3>

          <p>
            This is the most overlooked spec in the 10kW class, and it decides how much of the generator you can actually use. Power reaches your house through one 240V outlet, a cord and an inlet box. A 30-amp, 240V L14-30 circuit carries at most 7,200 watts, because 30 amps times 240 volts equals 7,200. A 50-amp 14-50 circuit carries up to 12,000 watts. On a generator rated for 8,000 or 9,000 running watts, the 30-amp outlet is a bottleneck.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Outlet</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Amps x volts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Max watts to the panel</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cord gauge (typical)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">L14-30R twist-lock</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30A x 240V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7,200W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-gauge</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">14-50R</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50A x 240V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-gauge</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Champion 14-50R (37.5A rated)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">37.5A x 240V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-gauge cord on a 50A inlet</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the GP8000E Ranks Last</h3>

          <p>
            That is the concrete reason the Generac GP8000E ranks last despite being a perfectly good generator. Its only 240V outlet is an L14-30R. Through a standard inlet and transfer switch it can never deliver more than 7,200 of its 8,000 running watts to your panel. For a well pump and essentials that may be enough. For a soft-started air conditioner plus essentials it is not. All four other picks carry a 14-50R, so they can feed a 50-amp inlet and use their full rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Hardware That Follows the Outlet</h3>

          <p>
            The outlet also sets the rest of the hardware. A 50-amp setup needs a 50-amp power inlet box, a 50-amp manual transfer switch or interlock with a 50-amp breaker, and a heavy 6-gauge cord. A 30-amp setup uses a lighter 10-gauge cord and cheaper switch gear. Our <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch guide</a> explains the installation options and the <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">best manual transfer switches</a> page lists 30A and 50A units. If your electrician is already installing a 30-amp inlet, a 50-amp one is usually only a modest step up in parts, and it future-proofs the setup.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a deeper comparison of generators built around the 50-amp outlet, see our guide to the <a href="/best-generator-with-50-amp-outlet/">best generator with a 50 amp outlet</a>. The short version for this class: if you are going to wire a 10kW generator into the house, buy one with a 14-50R, or you are paying for watts you cannot use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-burn" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Fuel Burn and Runtime at 10kW
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rated Runtimes</h3>

          <p>
            A 10kW generator is thirsty compared with a small inverter, and fuel logistics decide how long you can actually ride out an outage. Working from manufacturers' runtime ratings: the Champion 201417 is rated for 15 hours at quarter load on 6.08 gallons, about 0.4 gallons per hour. The Westinghouse iGen12000DFc is rated for 11 hours at half load on 7.9 gallons, about 0.7 gallons per hour. The DuroMax XP11000iH is rated for 9 hours at half load on 7.1 gallons, close to 0.8 gallons per hour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gallons per Outage Day</h3>

          <p>
            Translate that into an outage day. A realistic essentials load with a well pump and furnace averages somewhere between quarter and half load, so plan on roughly 10 to 15 gallons of gasoline per 24 hours of continuous running. Three days means 30 to 45 gallons, which is six to nine 5-gallon cans, all of which need stabilizer and rotation. That storage problem is the main reason people choose dual fuel. Our article on <a href="/how-long-can-a-generator-run-continuously/">how long a generator can run continuously</a> covers the oil-change intervals that also come due on long runs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane Runtime</h3>

          <p>
            Propane is easier to store and does not go stale, but a 20 lb cylinder holds only about 4.7 gallons. Champion rates the 201417 for 8 hours on one at quarter load; Westinghouse rates the iGen12000DFc for 5 hours at half load. That means three to five 20 lb cylinders per day on a 10kW unit. Many owners move to a 100 lb cylinder or a regulator and hose to a larger tank. Our <a href="/how-much-propane-does-a-generator-use/">propane consumption guide</a> has the per-hour math, and the <a href="/best-propane-generator/">best propane generator</a> page covers units built around LP.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rated runtime, from spec sheets</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">19 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">iGen12000DFc, 1/4 load, gas</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">18 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">XP11000iH, 1/4 load, gas</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Champion 201417, 1/4 load, gas</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Champion 201417, 20 lb LP</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Inverters Sip Fuel at Light Load</h3>

          <p>
            Inverters have a real advantage here. Their engines throttle down when the load drops, so overnight, when the house is drawing a refrigerator and a furnace blower, they sip fuel. Open-frame generators like the GP8000E run at a fixed 3,600 rpm regardless of load, which is why their quarter-load runtimes do not stretch the way an inverter's do. Over a week-long outage that difference can be dozens of gallons.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inverter-or-open-frame" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Inverter or Open Frame, Gas or Dual Fuel
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Inverters Dominate This Class</h3>

          <p>
            Ten years ago a 10,000 watt inverter generator was a commercial product. Now four of our five picks are inverters, and that reflects where the value is. An inverter costs more up front, but it runs quieter, burns less fuel at partial load and produces cleaner power. For a generator that will sit outside your house for days, the noise and fuel savings are not luxuries. Our <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter vs regular generator comparison</a> walks through the trade-offs in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Open Frame Still Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            An open-frame generator still makes sense in two cases. The first is budget: the Generac GP8000E typically costs far less than any 9,000 running-watt inverter, and if you need a name brand on a tight budget for occasional outages, it does the job. The second is rough use: open-frame units shrug off jobsite abuse, and their simpler electronics are easier to repair. Generac's support answers state that GP series generators run above 5 percent total harmonic distortion, while Champion rates the 201423 under 3 percent. Owners commonly run furnaces and refrigerators on GP-series power anyway, but sensitive electronics are a better match for inverter output.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dual Fuel at This Size</h3>

          <p>
            On fuel, we would buy dual fuel at this size unless there is a specific reason not to. Propane stores indefinitely, can be delivered, and does not gum up a carburetor over a summer of sitting. The gasoline-only GP8000E is the exception on this list. If you already have a natural gas line, the tri-fuel Champion 201423 removes refueling from the equation entirely, at the cost of about 1,700 running watts compared with gasoline. Our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">best dual fuel generator</a> roundup covers smaller dual fuel machines if 10kW turns out to be more than you need.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Placement and Carbon Monoxide</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, place it properly. A 10kW generator produces a lot of exhaust, and carbon monoxide shutoff systems like CO Shield, CO Alert and COsense are a backstop, not a substitute for distance. Keep it at least 20 feet from the house with the exhaust pointed away from doors and windows; our guide on <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> has the details.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="right-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Is 10kW the Right Size for You?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who 10kW Suits</h3>

          <p>
            A 10,000 watt generator is the sweet spot for a specific household: one with a 240V well pump, a gas furnace, a standard refrigerator and freezer, and perhaps a central air system it is willing to fit with a soft starter. For that house, 9,000 running watts covers essentially normal life minus simultaneous big appliances. It is also the largest size most people can still move without a trailer, since every pick here weighs between 189 and about 216 lb.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When It Is More Than You Need</h3>

          <p>
            If you are on city water with a gas furnace and no plans for air conditioning, it is likely more than you need. A 5,000 to 7,500 watt dual fuel generator will run your refrigerator, sump pump, furnace and lights for less money and less fuel. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/">generator sizing guide</a> walks through the calculation, and the <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generator</a> roundup covers those smaller sizes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Move Up</h3>

          <p>
            If you want central air without a soft starter, two big 240V loads at once, or a heat pump, move up. The <a href="/best-12000-watt-generator/">12,000 watt generator</a> class adds 1,000 to 3,000 running watts and true 50-amp panels, and beyond that a standby generator becomes the more sensible buy. A 10kW portable that strains every time the AC cycles is worse than a properly sized larger unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Our Ranking Reflects This</h3>

          <p>
            Our ranking reflects that reasoning. The Champion 201417 is the best all-rounder: 9,000 running watts, clean inverter power, a 50-amp-capable outlet and a reasonable noise rating. The DuroMax XP11000iH is the propane-first pick with a parallel path. The Westinghouse iGen12000DFc runs longest and weighs least. The Champion 201423 is for natural gas homes. The Generac GP8000E is the budget name brand, with a clear limit on what it can push into a panel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What will a 10,000 watt generator run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With 8,000 to 9,000 running watts, it runs a refrigerator, freezer, gas furnace, 1 HP 240V well pump, sump pump, lights, internet and a microwave together with margin to spare. It can also run one big 240V load, such as an electric dryer or water heater, alongside essentials. Central air usually needs a soft starter on the compressor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 10,000 watt generator run a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It runs most of a typical house, but not everything at once. Essentials plus one large appliance fit comfortably. A home with central air, an electric range, a dryer and an electric water heater running simultaneously needs more. Treat 10kW as near-normal living with the big 240V loads taken in turns.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 10,000 watt generator run central air?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes a two-ton system with little else running, but a three-ton unit can surge to 9,000 to 12,000 watts and will usually stall it. A soft starter fitted to the condenser commonly cuts that surge to 3,000 to 5,000 watts, which makes central air practical on every 9,000 running-watt pick in this guide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a 10,000 watt inverter generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The Champion 201417, DuroMax XP11000iH, Westinghouse iGen12000DFc and Champion 201423 are all inverter generators with about 9,000 running watts on gasoline and 120/240V output. They cost more than open-frame units but run quieter, burn less fuel at light loads and produce cleaner power for electronics.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much fuel does a 10,000 watt generator use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Based on manufacturers' runtime ratings, roughly 0.4 gallons per hour at quarter load on an inverter and 0.7 to 0.8 gallons per hour at half load. At a mixed outage load expect about 10 to 15 gallons of gasoline per day. On propane, a 20 lb cylinder lasts roughly 5 to 8 hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Generac make a 10,000 watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Generac GP8000E, sold in 50-state form as model 8929, is rated at 10,000 starting and 8,000 running watts. It is gasoline-only and its 240V output is a 30-amp L14-30R, which limits what it can feed through a transfer switch to 7,200 watts. Generac's standby generators cover larger home backup needs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 50 amp outlet on a 10,000 watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you are connecting it to the house through an inlet and transfer switch, yes. A 30-amp, 240V connection tops out at 7,200 watts, below the 8,000 to 9,000 running watts this class produces. A 14-50R outlet and a 50-amp inlet let you use the generator's full capacity.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the Peak Number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A generator sold as 12,000 watts can be a 9,000 running-watt machine, and one sold as 10,000 watts can run on 8,000. Running watts on the fuel you plan to use are the only figure that tells you what the generator sustains. Compare every candidate on that number and the classes become obvious.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pairing an 8,000W Generator With a 30A Inlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 30-amp, 240V inlet carries at most 7,200 watts. If you buy a 9,000 running-watt generator and connect it through a 30-amp cord and switch, you have paid for watts you cannot use. Choose a model with a 14-50R and install a 50-amp inlet from the start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting Central Air Without a Soft Starter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A three-ton compressor can surge to 9,000 to 12,000 watts on its own, which is everything a 10kW generator has. Fit a soft starter to the condenser before the outage, test it on generator power, and keep other big 240V loads off while the air conditioner runs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Two Big 240V Loads at Once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dryer, a water heater and an oven each draw several kilowatts. Any one of them fits on a 10kW generator alongside essentials, but two together usually overload it. Rotate them: heat water, then switch it off, then dry clothes, and cook on burners instead of the oven.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating Fuel for a Long Outage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 10kW generator commonly burns 10 to 15 gallons of gasoline a day at moderate load, or three to five 20 lb propane cylinders. Plan fuel for the outage you fear, not the one you hope for, and keep gasoline stabilized and rotated so it is usable when you need it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

